<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-lattice-theory">
<title>Lattice Theory</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Relations and partial orders from <xref ref="sec-relations" />, elementary set theory
from <xref ref="sec-elementary-set-theory" />, abstract algebra from
<xref ref="sec-abstract-algebra" />, and universal algebra from
<xref ref="sec-universal-algebra" />.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>distinguish partially ordered sets from lattices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify comparable and incomparable elements;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>construct and interpret Hasse diagrams;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>determine minimal, maximal, least, and greatest elements;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>determine upper bounds, lower bounds, suprema, and infima;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define lattices using both order-theoretic and algebraic viewpoints;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute meets and joins;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize chains, antichains, sublattices, and product lattices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish bounded, complemented, distributive, and modular lattices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>work with complete lattices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand Boolean algebras as complemented distributive lattices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>define lattice homomorphisms and lattice isomorphisms;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>interpret congruence lattices;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>state the Knaster--Tarski fixed-point theorem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>explain connections with algebra, logic, topology, and computer science.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-from-order-to-lattice">
<title>From Order to Lattice</title>
<p>A partially ordered set provides a way to compare elements without requiring
every pair to be comparable.</p>

<p>Lattice theory begins with this order structure and asks a stronger question:</p>

<p>\begin{quote}
Given two elements, is there a best element above both and a best element
below both?
\end{quote}</p>

<p>When both always exist, the partially ordered set is a lattice.</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
A <em>lattice</em> is an ordered structure in which every pair of elements
has both a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound.
\end{conceptbox}</p>

<p>Lattices appear throughout mathematics because many different notions of
<q>combining upward</q> and <q>combining downward</q> satisfy this pattern.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>union and intersection of sets;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>greatest common divisors and least common multiples;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>logical OR and AND;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>subgroups ordered by inclusion;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>subspaces ordered by inclusion;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>divisibility among integers;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>congruence relations on an algebra.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-partial-orders">
<title>Review of Partial Orders</title>
<p>A relation <m>\leq</m> on a set <m>P</m> is a partial order if it is reflexive,
antisymmetric, and transitive.</p>

<p>Thus:</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq x,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\text{ and }y\leq x\Longrightarrow x=y,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\text{ and }y\leq z\Longrightarrow x\leq z.</md>
</p>

<p>The pair</p>

<p>
<md>(P,\leq)</md>
</p>

<p>is called a <em>partially ordered set</em>, or <em>poset</em>.</p>

<p>The notation is read <q>@@PROTECTED0@@ partially ordered by less than or equal to.</q></p>

<p>The symbol <m>\leq</m> is abstract here. It need not represent ordinary
numerical inequality.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-comparable-incomparable">
<title>Comparable and Incomparable Elements</title>
<p>Two elements <m>x,y\in P</m> are <em>comparable</em> if</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\qquad\text{or}\qquad y\leq x.</md>
</p>

<p>Otherwise they are <em>incomparable</em>.</p>

<p>Incomparability is often denoted</p>

<p>
<md>x\parallel y.</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol <m>\parallel</m> is read here as <q>@@PROTECTED1@@ is incomparable with
@@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>For example, under divisibility,</p>

<p>
<md>2\mid6,</md>
</p>

<p>so <m>2</m> and <m>6</m> are comparable.</p>

<p>However,</p>

<p>
<md>2\nmid3,\qquad3\nmid2,</md>
</p>

<p>so <m>2</m> and <m>3</m> are incomparable.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-chains-antichains">
<title>Chains and Antichains</title>
<definition>
<title>Chain</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>chain</em> in a poset is a subset in which every pair of elements is
comparable.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus a chain behaves like a totally ordered subset.</p>

<definition>
<title>Antichain</title>
<statement>
<p>An <em>antichain</em> is a subset in which no two distinct elements are
comparable.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>For the power set</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal P(\{1,2,3\})</md>
</p>

<p>ordered by inclusion,</p>

<p>
<md>\varnothing\subseteq\{1\}\subseteq\{1,2\}\subseteq\{1,2,3\}</md>
</p>

<p>is a chain, while</p>

<p>
<md>\bigl\{\{1\},\{2\},\{3\}\bigr\}</md>
</p>

<p>is an antichain.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-covering-relations">
<title>Covering Relations</title>
<definition>
<title>Cover</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>x&lt;y</m> in a poset. We say that <m>y</m> <em>covers</em> <m>x</m> if there is no
element <m>z</m> satisfying</p>

<p>
<md>x&lt;z&lt;y.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A covering relation records an immediate step upward in the order.</p>

<p>One commonly writes</p>

<p>
<md>x\prec y.</md>
</p>

<p>The symbol <m>\prec</m> is read <q>@@PROTECTED1@@ is covered by @@PROTECTED2@@</q> in this context.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-hasse-diagrams">
<title>Hasse Diagrams</title>
<p>A finite poset can often be represented by a <em>Hasse diagram</em>.</p>

<p>In a Hasse diagram:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>larger elements are placed above smaller elements;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>an edge connects <m>x</m> to <m>y</m> when <m>y</m> covers <m>x</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>arrows are usually omitted because upward direction indicates order;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>reflexive and transitive edges are omitted.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>For example, consider the divisors of <m>12</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\{1,2,3,4,6,12\},</md>
</p>

<p>ordered by divisibility.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=12mm and 16mm,
    every node/.style={draw,circle,minimum size=8mm,inner sep=1pt}
]
\node (one) {<m>1</m>};
\node[above left=of one] (two) {<m>2</m>};
\node[above right=of one] (three) {<m>3</m>};
\node[above left=of two] (four) {<m>4</m>};
\node[above right=of two] (six) {<m>6</m>};
\node[above=of six] (twelve) {<m>12</m>};</p>

<p>\draw (one)--(two);
\draw (one)--(three);
\draw (two)--(four);
\draw (two)--(six);
\draw (three)--(six);
\draw (four)--(twelve);
\draw (six)--(twelve);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>The diagram encodes the divisibility order without drawing every implied
relation.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-minimal-maximal">
<title>Minimal and Maximal Elements</title>
<definition>
<title>Minimal Element</title>
<statement>
<p>An element <m>m\in P</m> is <em>minimal</em> if there is no <m>x\in P</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>x&lt;m.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<definition>
<title>Maximal Element</title>
<statement>
<p>An element <m>m\in P</m> is <em>maximal</em> if there is no <m>x\in P</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>m&lt;x.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A poset may have several minimal or maximal elements.</p>

<p>This differs from least and greatest elements.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-least-greatest-elements">
<title>Least and Greatest Elements</title>
<definition>
<title>Least Element</title>
<statement>
<p>An element <m>0\in P</m> is a <em>least element</em> if</p>

<p>
<md>0\leq x</md>
</p>

<p>for every <m>x\in P</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<definition>
<title>Greatest Element</title>
<statement>
<p>An element <m>1\in P</m> is a <em>greatest element</em> if</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq1</md>
</p>

<p>for every <m>x\in P</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The symbols <m>0</m> and <m>1</m> are conventional lattice notation and do not
necessarily represent numerical zero and one.</p>

<p>A least element is automatically the unique minimal element.</p>

<p>A greatest element is automatically the unique maximal element.</p>

<p>The converses need not hold.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-minimal-not-least">
<title>Minimal Does Not Mean Least</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=14mm and 18mm,
    every node/.style={draw,circle,minimum size=8mm}
]
\node (a) {<m>a</m>};
\node[right=of a] (b) {<m>b</m>};
\node[above=of $(a)!0.5!(b)$] (c) {<m>c</m>};</p>

<p>\draw (a)--(c);
\draw (b)--(c);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>Both <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are minimal.</p>

<p>Neither is least because neither lies below the other.</p>

<p>The element <m>c</m> is greatest.</p>

<p>This distinction is fundamental in order theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-upper-lower-bounds">
<title>Upper and Lower Bounds</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>S\subseteq P.</md>
</p>

<definition>
<title>Upper Bound</title>
<statement>
<p>An element <m>u\in P</m> is an <em>upper bound</em> of <m>S</m> if</p>

<p>
<md>s\leq u</md>
</p>

<p>for every <m>s\in S</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<definition>
<title>Lower Bound</title>
<statement>
<p>An element <m>\ell\in P</m> is a <em>lower bound</em> of <m>S</m> if</p>

<p>
<md>\ell\leq s</md>
</p>

<p>for every <m>s\in S</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>An upper or lower bound need not belong to <m>S</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-suprema-infima-lattice">
<title>Suprema and Infima</title>
<definition>
<title>Supremum</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>supremum</em> of <m>S</m>, if it exists, is the least upper bound of
<m>S</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>It is commonly written</p>

<p>
<md>\sup S.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read <q>the supremum of @@PROTECTED0@@</q> or <q>the least upper bound of @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Infimum</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>infimum</em> of <m>S</m>, if it exists, is the greatest lower bound of
<m>S</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>It is written</p>

<p>
<md>\inf S.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read <q>the infimum of @@PROTECTED0@@</q> or <q>the greatest lower bound of
@@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>These notions later play a major role in real analysis.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-join-meet">
<title>Join and Meet</title>
<p>For two elements <m>x,y</m>, lattice theory uses special notation for their
least upper bound and greatest lower bound.</p>

<definition>
<title>Join</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>join</em> of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> is their least upper bound:</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee y=\sup\{x,y\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The symbol <m>\vee</m> is read <q>join.</q></p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee y</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>@@PROTECTED0@@ join @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<definition>
<title>Meet</title>
<statement>
<p>The <em>meet</em> of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> is their greatest lower bound:</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y=\inf\{x,y\}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The symbol <m>\wedge</m> is read <q>meet.</q></p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>@@PROTECTED0@@ meet @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-visual-meet-join">
<title>Visual Meaning of Meet and Join</title>
<p>Suppose a lattice contains elements arranged as</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=12mm and 18mm,
    every node/.style={draw,circle,minimum size=10mm}
]
\node (meet) {<m>x\wedge y</m>};
\node[above left=of meet] (x) {<m>x</m>};
\node[above right=of meet] (y) {<m>y</m>};
\node[above=22mm of meet] (join) {<m>x\vee y</m>};</p>

<p>\draw (meet)--(x);
\draw (meet)--(y);
\draw (x)--(join);
\draw (y)--(join);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>The meet is the highest element lying below both <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.</p>

<p>The join is the lowest element lying above both <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-order-lattice-definition">
<title>Order-Theoretic Definition of a Lattice</title>
<definition>
<title>Lattice</title>
<statement>
<p>A poset <m>(L,\leq)</m> is a <em>lattice</em> if every pair <m>x,y\in L</m> has a
meet and a join.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Equivalently, for every <m>x,y\in L</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y\quad\text{and}\quad x\vee y</md>
</p>

<p>must exist.</p>

<p>The requirement concerns pairs of elements, not necessarily arbitrary
subsets.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-poset-not-lattice">
<title>A Poset That Is Not a Lattice</title>
<p>Consider</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=14mm and 20mm,
    every node/.style={draw,circle,minimum size=8mm}
]
\node (a) {<m>a</m>};
\node[right=of a] (b) {<m>b</m>};
\node[above=of a] (c) {<m>c</m>};
\node[above=of b] (d) {<m>d</m>};</p>

<p>\draw (a)--(c);
\draw (b)--(d);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>The elements <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> have no common upper bound.</p>

<p>Therefore <m>a\vee b</m> does not exist.</p>

<p>The poset is not a lattice.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-power-set-lattice">
<title>The Power-Set Lattice</title>
<p>Let <m>X</m> be a set.</p>

<p>The power set</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal P(X)</md>
</p>

<p>ordered by inclusion is a lattice.</p>

<p>For subsets <m>A,B\subseteq X</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>A\wedge B=A\cap B, \qquad A\vee B=A\cup B.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus intersection acts as meet and union acts as join.</p>

<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X=\{1,2,3\},</md>
</p>

<p>the lattice has the Hasse diagram</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=11mm and 13mm,
    every node/.style={draw,rounded corners,inner sep=3pt,font=\small}
]
\node (empty) {<m>\varnothing</m>};</p>

<p>\node[above left=of empty] (one) {<m>\{1\}</m>};
\node[above=of empty] (two) {<m>\{2\}</m>};
\node[above right=of empty] (three) {<m>\{3\}</m>};</p>

<p>\node[above left=15mm and -2mm of two] (onetwo) {<m>\{1,2\}</m>};
\node[above=15mm of two] (onethree) {<m>\{1,3\}</m>};
\node[above right=15mm and -2mm of two] (twothree) {<m>\{2,3\}</m>};</p>

<p>\node[above=15mm of onethree] (all) {<m>\{1,2,3\}</m>};</p>

<p>\draw (empty)--(one);
\draw (empty)--(two);
\draw (empty)--(three);</p>

<p>\draw (one)--(onetwo);
\draw (one)--(onethree);</p>

<p>\draw (two)--(onetwo);
\draw (two)--(twothree);</p>

<p>\draw (three)--(onethree);
\draw (three)--(twothree);</p>

<p>\draw (onetwo)--(all);
\draw (onethree)--(all);
\draw (twothree)--(all);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>This lattice is often denoted</p>

<p>
<md>B_3.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-divisibility-lattices">
<title>Divisibility Lattices</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>D_n=\{d\in\N:d\mid n\}</md>
</p>

<p>be the set of positive divisors of <m>n</m>, ordered by divisibility.</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>a\wedge b=\gcd(a,b),</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>a\vee b=\operatorname{lcm}(a,b).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus greatest common divisor and least common multiple are lattice
operations.</p>

<p>For example, among divisors of <m>60</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>12\wedge20=\gcd(12,20)=4,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>12\vee20=\operatorname{lcm}(12,20)=60.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-worked-meet-join">
<title>Worked Example: Meet and Join</title>
<example>
<title>Meet and Join in a Divisibility Lattice</title>
<p>Consider the divisors of <m>36</m>, ordered by divisibility. Determine the meet
and join of <m>6</m> and <m>9</m>.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{6\wedge9=3,\qquad6\vee9=18.}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-subspace-lattices">
<title>Subspace Lattices</title>
<p>Let <m>V</m> be a vector space.</p>

<p>The collection of subspaces of <m>V</m>, ordered by inclusion, forms a lattice.</p>

<p>For subspaces <m>U,W\leq V</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>U\wedge W=U\cap W,</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>U\vee W=U+W.</md>
</p>

<p>The sum <m>U+W</m> is the smallest subspace containing both <m>U</m> and <m>W</m>.</p>

<p>This gives an important connection between lattice theory and linear
algebra.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-subgroup-lattices">
<title>Subgroup Lattices</title>
<p>For a group <m>G</m>, the set of subgroups</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Sub}(G)</md>
</p>

<p>ordered by inclusion forms a lattice.</p>

<p>For subgroups <m>H,K\leq G</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>H\wedge K=H\cap K.</md>
</p>

<p>The join is the subgroup generated by both:</p>

<p>
<md>H\vee K=\langle H\cup K\rangle.</md>
</p>

<p>It is important that <m>H\cup K</m> itself is generally not a subgroup.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-algebraic-lattice-definition">
<title>The Algebraic Definition of a Lattice</title>
<p>A lattice can also be defined without explicitly mentioning an order.</p>

<definition>
<title>Lattice Algebra</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>lattice algebra</em> is a set <m>L</m> equipped with two binary operations
<m>\wedge</m> and <m>\vee</m> satisfying the commutative, associative,
idempotent, and absorption identities.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>These identities are</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y=y\wedge x,\qquad
x\vee y=y\vee x,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>(x\wedge y)\wedge z=x\wedge(y\wedge z),\qquad
(x\vee y)\vee z=x\vee(y\vee z),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge x=x,\qquad x\vee x=x,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge(x\vee y)=x,\qquad
x\vee(x\wedge y)=x.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-absorption-laws">
<title>Absorption Laws</title>
<p>The identities</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge(x\vee y)=x</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee(x\wedge y)=x</md>
</p>

<p>are called the <em>absorption laws</em>.</p>

<p>They encode the relationship between meet and join.</p>

<p>If <m>x\vee y</m> already lies above <m>x</m>, then meeting it back with <m>x</m>
returns <m>x</m>.</p>

<p>Dually, if <m>x\wedge y</m> lies below <m>x</m>, joining it with <m>x</m> returns
<m>x</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-order-from-meet-join">
<title>Order from Meet and Join</title>
<p>Given a lattice algebra, define</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\iff x\wedge y=x.</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\iff x\vee y=y.</md>
</p>

<p>These conditions recover the underlying partial order.</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}
For a lattice,</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y
\iff x\wedge y=x
\iff x\vee y=y.</md>
</p>

<p>\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This establishes the equivalence between the order-theoretic and algebraic
definitions of a lattice.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-duality">
<title>Duality Principle</title>
<p>Lattice theory possesses a powerful symmetry.</p>

<p>If every order relation is reversed, then meets and joins exchange roles.</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\leq\longleftrightarrow\geq, \qquad
\wedge\longleftrightarrow\vee, \qquad
0\longleftrightarrow1.</md>
</p>

<p>\begin{principle}[Principle of Duality]
If a statement about lattices follows from the lattice axioms, then the
dual statement obtained by interchanging meet and join and reversing the
order also holds.
\end{principle}</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge(x\vee y)=x</md>
</p>

<p>has dual</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee(x\wedge y)=x.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-sublattices">
<title>Sublattices</title>
<definition>
<title>Sublattice</title>
<statement>
<p>A nonempty subset <m>S\subseteq L</m> is a <em>sublattice</em> if it is closed
under meet and join.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus for every <m>x,y\in S</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y\in S,\qquad x\vee y\in S.</md>
</p>

<p>A subset can be a subposet without being a sublattice.</p>

<p>The additional requirement is closure under the lattice operations.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-generated-sublattices">
<title>Generated Sublattices</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>X\subseteq L,</md>
</p>

<p>the smallest sublattice containing <m>X</m> is denoted</p>

<p>
<md>\langle X\rangle_{\mathrm{Lat}}.</md>
</p>

<p>It is obtained by repeatedly applying meet and join to elements of <m>X</m>.</p>

<p>This is a special case of the generated-subalgebra construction from
universal algebra.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-product-lattices">
<title>Product Lattices</title>
<p>If <m>L</m> and <m>M</m> are lattices, their Cartesian product</p>

<p>
<md>L\times M</md>
</p>

<p>becomes a lattice under coordinatewise order:</p>

<p>
<md>(x_1,y_1)\leq(x_2,y_2)
\iff x_1\leq x_2\text{ and }y_1\leq y_2.</md>
</p>

<p>Meet and join are computed coordinatewise:</p>

<p>
<md>(x_1,y_1)\wedge(x_2,y_2)
=(x_1\wedge x_2,\ y_1\wedge y_2),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>(x_1,y_1)\vee(x_2,y_2)
=(x_1\vee x_2,\ y_1\vee y_2).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-bounded-lattices">
<title>Bounded Lattices</title>
<definition>
<title>Bounded Lattice</title>
<statement>
<p>A lattice <m>L</m> is <em>bounded</em> if it contains both a least element <m>0</m>
and a greatest element <m>1</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>0\leq x\leq1</md>
</p>

<p>for every <m>x\in L</m>.</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>0\wedge x=0,\qquad0\vee x=x,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>1\wedge x=x,\qquad1\vee x=1.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-complements">
<title>Complements</title>
<definition>
<title>Complement</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>L</m> be a bounded lattice. An element <m>y</m> is a <em>complement</em> of
<m>x</m> if</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y=0,\qquad x\vee y=1.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>A complement is often written</p>

<p>
<md>x'.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read <q>@@PROTECTED0@@ complement.</q></p>

<p>Depending on the lattice, an element may have:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>no complement;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>exactly one complement;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>several complements.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-complemented-lattices">
<title>Complemented Lattices</title>
<definition>
<title>Complemented Lattice</title>
<statement>
<p>A bounded lattice is <em>complemented</em> if every element has at least one
complement.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>In the power-set lattice,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal P(X),</md>
</p>

<p>the complement of <m>A\subseteq X</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>A^c=X\setminus A.</md>
</p>

<p>Indeed,</p>

<p>
<md>A\cap A^c=\varnothing, \qquad A\cup A^c=X.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-distributive-lattices">
<title>Distributive Lattices</title>
<definition>
<title>Distributive Lattice</title>
<statement>
<p>A lattice is <em>distributive</em> if</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge(y\vee z)
=(x\wedge y)\vee(x\wedge z)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee(y\wedge z)
=(x\vee y)\wedge(x\vee z)</md>
</p>

<p>for all <m>x,y,z\in L</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>By lattice duality, either distributive identity implies the other in a
lattice.</p>

<p>The power-set lattice is distributive because set intersection and union
satisfy the familiar distributive laws.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-diamond-lattice">
<title>The Diamond Lattice <m>M_3</m></title>
<p>A standard nondistributive lattice is the five-element diamond lattice
<m>M_3</m>.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=13mm and 15mm,
    every node/.style={draw,circle,minimum size=8mm}
]
\node (zero) {<m>0</m>};
\node[above left=of zero] (a) {<m>a</m>};
\node[above=of zero] (b) {<m>b</m>};
\node[above right=of zero] (c) {<m>c</m>};
\node[above=26mm of zero] (one) {<m>1</m>};</p>

<p>\draw (zero)--(a);
\draw (zero)--(b);
\draw (zero)--(c);
\draw (a)--(one);
\draw (b)--(one);
\draw (c)--(one);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>For distinct <m>a,b,c</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>b\vee c=1.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore</p>

<p>
<md>a\wedge(b\vee c)=a.</md>
</p>

<p>But</p>

<p>
<md>a\wedge b=0,\qquad a\wedge c=0,</md>
</p>

<p>so</p>

<p>
<md>(a\wedge b)\vee(a\wedge c)=0.</md>
</p>

<p>Hence distributivity fails.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-pentagon-lattice">
<title>The Pentagon Lattice <m>N_5</m></title>
<p>Another standard nondistributive lattice is <m>N_5</m>.</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=13mm and 18mm,
    every node/.style={draw,circle,minimum size=8mm}
]
\node (zero) {<m>0</m>};
\node[above left=of zero] (a) {<m>a</m>};
\node[above=of a] (b) {<m>b</m>};
\node[above right=of zero] (c) {<m>c</m>};
\node[above right=of b] (one) {<m>1</m>};</p>

<p>\draw (zero)--(a);
\draw (a)--(b);
\draw (b)--(one);
\draw (zero)--(c);
\draw (c)--(one);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>The shape resembles a pentagon, which motivates its name.</p>

<p>The lattices <m>M_3</m> and <m>N_5</m> characterize failure of distributivity in
an important theorem.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-distributive-characterization">
<title>Characterization of Distributive Lattices</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Birkhoff's Characterization]
A lattice is distributive if and only if it contains no sublattice
isomorphic to <m>M_3</m> or <m>N_5</m>.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Thus the two small lattices <m>M_3</m> and <m>N_5</m> are the fundamental
obstructions to distributivity.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-modular-lattices">
<title>Modular Lattices</title>
<p>Distributivity is a strong condition. A weaker condition is modularity.</p>

<definition>
<title>Modular Lattice</title>
<statement>
<p>A lattice <m>L</m> is <em>modular</em> if whenever <m>x\leq z</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee(y\wedge z)=(x\vee y)\wedge z.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The condition <m>x\leq z</m> is essential.</p>

<p>Every distributive lattice is modular.</p>

<p>The converse is false.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-distributive-versus-modular">
<title>Distributive Versus Modular</title>
<p>The relationship is</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=12mm,
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners,align=center,inner sep=6pt},
    &gt;=stealth
]
\node[box] (lat) {Lattices};
\node[box,below=of lat] (mod) {Modular lattices};
\node[box,below=of mod] (dist) {Distributive lattices};</p>

<p>\draw[-&gt;] (dist)--node[right]{implies}(mod);
\draw[-&gt;] (mod)--node[right]{special case of}(lat);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>The diamond <m>M_3</m> is modular but not distributive.</p>

<p>The pentagon <m>N_5</m> is not modular.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-subspace-modular">
<title>Subspace Lattices Are Modular</title>
<p>The lattice of subspaces of a vector space is generally modular.</p>

<p>If <m>X\subseteq Z</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>X+(Y\cap Z)=(X+Y)\cap Z.</md>
</p>

<p>In lattice notation,</p>

<p>
<md>X\vee(Y\wedge Z)=(X\vee Y)\wedge Z.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus linear algebra provides one of the most important examples of modular
lattices.</p>

<p>The subspace lattice need not be distributive.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-modular-characterization">
<title>Modular Characterization</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}
A lattice is modular if and only if it contains no sublattice isomorphic to
<m>N_5</m>.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Compare this with distributivity:</p>

<p>
<md>\text{distributive}
\iff\text{contains neither }M_3\text{ nor }N_5,</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\text{modular}
\iff\text{contains no }N_5.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-boolean-algebras">
<title>Boolean Algebras</title>
<definition>
<title>Boolean Algebra</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>Boolean algebra</em> is a bounded distributive lattice in which every
element has a complement.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus Boolean algebra combines:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>meet;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>join;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>least and greatest elements;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distributivity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>complementation.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>The standard example is</p>

<p>
<md>\bigl(\mathcal P(X),\cap,\cup,{}^c,\varnothing,X\bigr).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-boolean-operations">
<title>Boolean Operations</title>
<p>The same Boolean structure appears in several settings.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>This common structure explains the importance of Boolean algebra in logic,
set theory, digital circuits, and computer science.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-demorgan">
<title>De Morgan's Laws</title>
<p>In a Boolean algebra,</p>

<p>
<md>(x\wedge y)'=x'\vee y',</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>(x\vee y)'=x'\wedge y'.</md>
</p>

<p>These are <em>De Morgan's laws</em>.</p>

<p>They generalize the familiar set identities</p>

<p>
<md>(A\cap B)^c=A^c\cup B^c,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>(A\cup B)^c=A^c\cap B^c.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-unique-complements">
<title>Uniqueness of Complements</title>
<p>In an arbitrary complemented lattice, complements need not be unique.</p>

<p>In a distributive complemented lattice, however, they are unique.</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}
Every element of a Boolean algebra has a unique complement.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>\begin{proof}
Suppose <m>y</m> and <m>z</m> are complements of <m>x</m>. Then</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y=0,\qquad x\vee y=1,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge z=0,\qquad x\vee z=1.</md>
</p>

<p>Using distributivity,</p>

<p>
<md>\begin{aligned}
y
&amp;=y\wedge1\\
&amp;=y\wedge(x\vee z)\\
&amp;=(y\wedge x)\vee(y\wedge z)\\
&amp;=0\vee(y\wedge z)\\
&amp;=y\wedge z.
\end{aligned}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>z=y\wedge z.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore <m>y=z</m>.
\end{proof}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-complete-lattices">
<title>Complete Lattices</title>
<p>A lattice guarantees meets and joins for pairs.</p>

<p>A complete lattice requires them for arbitrary subsets.</p>

<definition>
<title>Complete Lattice</title>
<statement>
<p>A lattice <m>L</m> is <em>complete</em> if every subset <m>S\subseteq L</m> has both
a supremum and an infimum in <m>L</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\bigvee S</md>
</p>

<p>denotes the join or supremum of all elements of <m>S</m>, while</p>

<p>
<md>\bigwedge S</md>
</p>

<p>denotes their meet or infimum.</p>

<p>These are read <q>the join over @@PROTECTED0@@</q> and <q>the meet over @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-empty-meets-joins">
<title>Empty Meets and Joins</title>
<p>In a complete lattice, even the empty subset must have a supremum and an
infimum.</p>

<p>The conventions are</p>

<p>
<md>\bigvee\varnothing=0, \qquad \bigwedge\varnothing=1.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore every complete lattice is bounded.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-powerset-complete">
<title>Power Sets Are Complete Lattices</title>
<p>For any set <m>X</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal P(X)</md>
</p>

<p>is a complete lattice under inclusion.</p>

<p>For any family</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal A\subseteq\mathcal P(X),</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>\bigvee\mathcal A=\bigcup_{A\in\mathcal A}A,</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\bigwedge\mathcal A=\bigcap_{A\in\mathcal A}A.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus arbitrary unions and intersections provide arbitrary joins and meets.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-finite-lattice-completeness">
<title>Finite Lattices and Completeness</title>
<p>A finite lattice is complete if it is bounded.</p>

<p>In particular, every finite nonempty lattice has joins and meets of every
nonempty finite subset.</p>

<p>If it also possesses <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>, then the empty join and meet exist as
well.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-homomorphisms">
<title>Lattice Homomorphisms</title>
<definition>
<title>Lattice Homomorphism</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>L</m> and <m>M</m> be lattices. A function</p>

<p>
<md>f:L\to M</md>
</p>

<p>is a <em>lattice homomorphism</em> if</p>

<p>
<md>f(x\wedge y)=f(x)\wedge f(y)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>f(x\vee y)=f(x)\vee f(y)</md>
</p>

<p>for all <m>x,y\in L</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus a lattice homomorphism preserves both fundamental lattice operations.</p>

<p>For bounded lattices, one may additionally require</p>

<p>
<md>f(0)=0,\qquad f(1)=1,</md>
</p>

<p>depending on the chosen signature.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-hom-preserve-order">
<title>Lattice Homomorphisms Preserve Order</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}
Every lattice homomorphism is order-preserving.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>\begin{proof}
Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>x=x\wedge y.</md>
</p>

<p>Applying <m>f</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=f(x\wedge y)=f(x)\wedge f(y).</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)\leq f(y).</md>
</p>

<p>\end{proof}</p>

<p>The converse is not generally true: an order-preserving map need not
preserve meets and joins.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-isomorphisms">
<title>Lattice Isomorphisms</title>
<p>A bijective lattice homomorphism is a <em>lattice isomorphism</em>.</p>

<p>If <m>L</m> and <m>M</m> are isomorphic, write</p>

<p>
<md>L\cong M.</md>
</p>

<p>An isomorphism preserves the complete lattice structure, including order,
meet, and join.</p>

<p>For finite lattices, an isomorphism corresponds to an order-preserving
relabeling of the Hasse diagram.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-congruences">
<title>Congruences on Lattices</title>
<p>Because a lattice is an algebra with operations <m>\wedge</m> and <m>\vee</m>, a
lattice congruence is an equivalence relation compatible with both.</p>

<p>Thus if</p>

<p>
<md>x\mathrel{\theta}x',\qquad y\mathrel{\theta}y',</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y\mathrel{\theta}x'\wedge y',</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee y\mathrel{\theta}x'\vee y'.</md>
</p>

<p>The quotient</p>

<p>
<md>L/\theta</md>
</p>

<p>is again a lattice.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-congruence-lattices">
<title>Congruence Lattices</title>
<p>As introduced in universal algebra, the set of congruences on an algebra
forms a lattice.</p>

<p>For a lattice <m>L</m>, write</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Con}(L)</md>
</p>

<p>for its congruence lattice.</p>

<p>The ordering is inclusion.</p>

<p>The meet of two congruences is their intersection:</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha\wedge\beta=\alpha\cap\beta.</md>
</p>

<p>Their join is the smallest congruence containing both:</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha\vee\beta=\operatorname{Cg}(\alpha\cup\beta).</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-congruence-distributive">
<title>A Remarkable Congruence Theorem</title>
<p>Lattices possess an important universal-algebraic property.</p>

<p>\begin{theorem}
For every lattice <m>L</m>, the congruence lattice</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{Con}(L)</md>
</p>

<p>is distributive.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>Therefore the variety of lattices is <em>congruence distributive</em>.</p>

<p>This is a deeper property than saying that the original lattice <m>L</m> is
distributive.</p>

<p>Indeed, <m>L</m> itself may be nondistributive while <m>\operatorname{Con}(L)</m>
is always distributive.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-ideals">
<title>Ideals of a Lattice</title>
<p>The word <em>ideal</em> has a lattice-theoretic meaning related to, but
distinct from, ring ideals.</p>

<definition>
<title>Lattice Ideal</title>
<statement>
<p>A nonempty subset <m>I\subseteq L</m> is a <em>lattice ideal</em> if:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>whenever <m>x\in I</m> and <m>y\leq x</m>, then <m>y\in I</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>whenever <m>x,y\in I</m>, then <m>x\vee y\in I</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus an ideal is downward closed and closed under finite joins.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-filters">
<title>Filters</title>
<p>The dual concept is a filter.</p>

<definition>
<title>Filter</title>
<statement>
<p>A nonempty subset <m>F\subseteq L</m> is a <em>filter</em> if:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>whenever <m>x\in F</m> and <m>x\leq y</m>, then <m>y\in F</m>;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>whenever <m>x,y\in F</m>, then <m>x\wedge y\in F</m>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus filters are upward closed and closed under finite meets.</p>

<p>The ideal/filter relationship is another manifestation of lattice duality.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-principal-lattice-ideals">
<title>Principal Ideals and Filters</title>
<p>For <m>a\in L</m>, the <em>principal ideal</em> generated by <m>a</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\downarrow a=\{x\in L:x\leq a\}.</md>
</p>

<p>The downward arrow <m>\downarrow</m> is read <q>down-set generated by @@PROTECTED1@@</q>
or <q>principal ideal below @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>

<p>The principal filter generated by <m>a</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\uparrow a=\{x\in L:a\leq x\}.</md>
</p>

<p>The upward arrow <m>\uparrow</m> is read <q>up-set generated by @@PROTECTED1@@</q> or
<q>principal filter above @@PROTECTED2@@.</q></p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-of-ideals">
<title>The Lattice of Ideals</title>
<p>The collection of ideals of a lattice can itself be ordered by inclusion.</p>

<p>Under suitable definitions, this produces another lattice.</p>

<p>This recurring phenomenon is characteristic of lattice theory:</p>

<p>\begin{conceptbox}
Mathematical structures often generate new lattices consisting of their
substructures, congruences, ideals, closed sets, or other ordered families.
\end{conceptbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-closure-operators">
<title>Closure Operators</title>
<definition>
<title>Closure Operator</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>closure operator</em> on a poset <m>P</m> is a function</p>

<p>
<md>c:P\to P</md>
</p>

<p>satisfying:</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq c(x),
\qquad
x\leq y\Longrightarrow c(x)\leq c(y),
\qquad
c(c(x))=c(x).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>These properties are called:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>extensive;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>monotone;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>idempotent.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>Closure operators appear in topology, algebra, geometry, logic, and computer
science.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-closed-elements">
<title>Closed Elements</title>
<p>An element <m>x</m> is <em>closed</em> under <m>c</m> if</p>

<p>
<md>c(x)=x.</md>
</p>

<p>The set of fixed points of a closure operator often forms a complete
lattice.</p>

<p>Examples include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>closed subsets of a topological space;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>linear spans of subsets of a vector space;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>subgroups generated by subsets;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>algebraic closures in algebraic settings.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-monotone-maps">
<title>Monotone Maps</title>
<definition>
<title>Monotone Map</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> be posets. A function</p>

<p>
<md>f:P\to Q</md>
</p>

<p>is <em>monotone</em>, or <em>order-preserving</em>, if</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\Longrightarrow f(x)\leq f(y).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Monotone maps need not preserve meets or joins.</p>

<p>They are therefore more general than lattice homomorphisms.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-fixed-points">
<title>Fixed Points</title>
<p>A fixed point of a function</p>

<p>
<md>f:L\to L</md>
</p>

<p>is an element <m>x\in L</m> satisfying</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x.</md>
</p>

<p>Fixed points are important throughout mathematics.</p>

<p>Lattice theory provides powerful existence theorems when <m>L</m> is complete
and <m>f</m> is monotone.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-knaster-tarski">
<title>Knaster--Tarski Fixed-Point Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Knaster--Tarski Fixed-Point Theorem]
Let <m>L</m> be a complete lattice and let</p>

<p>
<md>f:L\to L</md>
</p>

<p>be monotone. Then the set of fixed points of <m>f</m> forms a complete lattice.
In particular, <m>f</m> has a least fixed point and a greatest fixed point.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>This is one of the most important applications of complete lattices.</p>

<p>It has major uses in:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>logic;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recursive definitions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>semantics of programming languages;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>game theory;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>dynamical constructions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>theoretical computer science.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-least-greatest-fixed-points">
<title>Least and Greatest Fixed Points</title>
<p>The least fixed point can be characterized by</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{lfp}(f)
=\bigwedge\{x\in L:f(x)\leq x\}.</md>
</p>

<p>Here</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{lfp}(f)</md>
</p>

<p>is read <q>the least fixed point of @@PROTECTED0@@.</q></p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\operatorname{gfp}(f)
=\bigvee\{x\in L:x\leq f(x)\}.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read <q>the greatest fixed point of @@PROTECTED0@@.</q></p>

<p>These formulas show how fixed-point theory is built directly from the meet
and join structure of a complete lattice.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-worked-lattice-fixed-point">
<title>Worked Example: A Fixed Point on a Power Set</title>
<example>
<title>Fixed Points of a Monotone Map</title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>X=\{1,2,3\}</md>
</p>

<p>and define</p>

<p>
<md>f:\mathcal P(X)\to\mathcal P(X),\qquad f(A)=A\cup\{1\}.</md>
</p>

<p>Determine the fixed points.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{\operatorname{Fix}(f)
=\bigl\{\{1\},\{1,2\},\{1,3\},\{1,2,3\}\bigr\}.}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-galois-connections">
<title>Galois Connections</title>
<p>The term <em>Galois connection</em> refers to an order-theoretic construction
that is broader than Galois theory of field extensions.</p>

<definition>
<title>Galois Connection</title>
<statement>
<p>Let <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> be posets. A pair of maps</p>

<p>
<md>f:P\to Q,\qquad g:Q\to P</md>
</p>

<p>forms a <em>Galois connection</em> if</p>

<p>
<md>f(p)\leq q\iff p\leq g(q).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The two maps determine one another through the order relation.</p>

<p>This idea appears in:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>algebra;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>topology;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>mathematical logic;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>formal concept analysis;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>category theory.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-adjoint-viewpoint">
<title>Adjoint Viewpoint</title>
<p>In a Galois connection,</p>

<p>
<md>f(p)\leq q\iff p\leq g(q),</md>
</p>

<p>the map <m>f</m> is called a <em>left adjoint</em> and <m>g</m> a <em>right
adjoint</em> in the order-theoretic sense.</p>

<p>This is the poset version of the categorical notion of adjunction developed
later in <xref ref="sec-category-theory" />.</p>

<p>A fundamental principle is:</p>

<p>\begin{importantbox}
Left adjoints preserve joins, while right adjoints preserve meets whenever
the relevant joins and meets exist.
\end{importantbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-antitone-maps">
<title>Order Reversal and Antitone Maps</title>
<p>A function <m>f:P\to Q</m> is <em>order-reversing</em>, or <em>antitone</em>, if</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\Longrightarrow f(y)\leq f(x).</md>
</p>

<p>Complementation in a Boolean algebra is order-reversing:</p>

<p>
<md>x\leq y\Longrightarrow y'\leq x'.</md>
</p>

<p>This reflects the familiar set-theoretic rule</p>

<p>
<md>A\subseteq B\Longrightarrow B^c\subseteq A^c.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-atoms-coatoms">
<title>Atoms and Coatoms</title>
<p>Let <m>L</m> be a bounded lattice.</p>

<definition>
<title>Atom</title>
<statement>
<p>An <em>atom</em> is an element <m>a\neq0</m> that covers <m>0</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Thus there is no element strictly between <m>0</m> and <m>a</m>.</p>

<p>Dually:</p>

<definition>
<title>Coatom</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>coatom</em> is an element <m>c\neq1</m> such that <m>1</m> covers <m>c</m>.</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>In the power-set lattice <m>\mathcal P(X)</m>, the atoms are the singleton
sets.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-atomic-lattices">
<title>Atomic Lattices</title>
<p>A bounded lattice is <em>atomic</em> if every nonzero element lies above an
atom.</p>

<p>A stronger condition sometimes used is <em>atomistic</em>: every element is
a join of atoms.</p>

<p>For a finite set <m>X</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal P(X)</md>
</p>

<p>is atomistic because every subset is the union, hence join, of its singleton
elements.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-intervals">
<title>Intervals</title>
<p>If <m>a\leq b</m> in a lattice <m>L</m>, define the interval</p>

<p>
<md>[a,b]=\{x\in L:a\leq x\leq b\}.</md>
</p>

<p>This is read <q>the interval from @@PROTECTED0@@ to @@PROTECTED1@@.</q></p>

<p>Intervals often inherit lattice structure from <m>L</m>.</p>

<p>In particular, if <m>x,y\in[a,b]</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y\in[a,b],\qquad x\vee y\in[a,b].</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-height">
<title>Finite Lattice Height</title>
<p>The <em>height</em> of a finite lattice is related to the length of its longest
chains.</p>

<p>A chain</p>

<p>
<md>x_0&lt;x_1&lt;\cdots&lt;x_n</md>
</p>

<p>has length <m>n</m>.</p>

<p>Depending on convention, the height of a lattice is the maximum chain length
or the number of levels. The convention should therefore always be stated
when exact numerical height matters.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-ranked-lattices">
<title>Ranked Lattices</title>
<p>A finite lattice may admit a rank function</p>

<p>
<md>\rho:L\to\N</md>
</p>

<p>such that covering relations increase rank by one.</p>

<p>The symbol</p>

<p>
<md>\rho</md>
</p>

<p>is the lowercase Greek letter <em>rho</em>, pronounced <q>row.</q></p>

<p>If <m>y</m> covers <m>x</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>\rho(y)=\rho(x)+1.</md>
</p>

<p>The Boolean lattice <m>B_n</m> is naturally ranked by</p>

<p>
<md>\rho(A)=|A|.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-boolean-lattice-levels">
<title>Boolean Lattice Levels</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>B_n=\mathcal P(\{1,\ldots,n\}),</md>
</p>

<p>the elements at rank <m>k</m> are precisely the <m>k</m>-element subsets.</p>

<p>Therefore the number of elements at level <m>k</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\binom{n}{k}.</md>
</p>

<p>This connects lattice theory directly with combinatorics.</p>

<p>The levels of <m>B_n</m> have sizes</p>

<p>
<md>\binom n0,\binom n1,\ldots,\binom nn.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattices-combinatorics">
<title>Lattices and Combinatorics</title>
<p>Finite posets and lattices are central objects in combinatorics.</p>

<p>Important questions include:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>maximum chain size;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>maximum antichain size;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>rank enumeration;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Möbius inversion;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>incidence algebras;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>order ideals;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distributive-lattice representations.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>These topics are developed more fully in the discrete mathematics and
combinatorics chapters.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-finite-distributive-lattices">
<title>Finite Distributive Lattices</title>
<p>Finite distributive lattices possess a particularly elegant representation.</p>

<p>Let <m>P</m> be a finite poset.</p>

<p>An <em>order ideal</em> of <m>P</m> is a subset <m>I\subseteq P</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>x\in I,\ y\leq x\Longrightarrow y\in I.</md>
</p>

<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>J(P)</md>
</p>

<p>denote the collection of order ideals of <m>P</m>.</p>

<p>Ordered by inclusion, <m>J(P)</m> is a distributive lattice.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-birkhoff-representation-lattices">
<title>Birkhoff Representation Theorem</title>
<p>\begin{theorem}[Birkhoff Representation Theorem]
Every finite distributive lattice is isomorphic to the lattice of order
ideals of a finite poset.
\end{theorem}</p>

<p>More specifically, if <m>L</m> is finite and distributive, it can be recovered
from the poset of its join-irreducible elements.</p>

<p>This theorem reveals that finite distributive lattices and finite posets are
closely intertwined.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-join-irreducible">
<title>Join-Irreducible Elements</title>
<definition>
<title>Join-Irreducible Element</title>
<statement>
<p>In a lattice with least element <m>0</m>, a nonzero element <m>j</m> is
<em>join-irreducible</em> if</p>

<p>
<md>j=x\vee y\Longrightarrow j=x\text{ or }j=y.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>Such an element cannot be decomposed nontrivially as a join of smaller
elements.</p>

<p>Dually, a <em>meet-irreducible</em> element cannot be decomposed nontrivially
as a meet.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-topology">
<title>Lattice Theory and Topology</title>
<p>For a topological space <m>X</m>, the collection of open sets</p>

<p>
<md>\mathcal O(X)</md>
</p>

<p>is ordered by inclusion.</p>

<p>Arbitrary unions of open sets are open, and finite intersections are open.</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\bigvee_{i\in I}U_i=\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i,</md>
</p>

<p>while finite meets are</p>

<p>
<md>U\wedge V=U\cap V.</md>
</p>

<p>The lattice of open sets is an example of a more specialized structure
called a <em>frame</em>.</p>

<p>This connects lattice theory with point-set topology and locale theory.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-frames">
<title>Frames</title>
<definition>
<title>Frame</title>
<statement>
<p>A <em>frame</em> is a complete lattice in which finite meets distribute over
arbitrary joins:</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge\left(\bigvee_{i\in I}y_i\right)
=\bigvee_{i\in I}(x\wedge y_i).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>The lattice of open sets of every topological space is a frame.</p>

<p>Frames allow aspects of topology to be studied algebraically.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-logic">
<title>Lattice Theory and Logic</title>
<p>Logical propositions can be ordered by implication.</p>

<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>P\Longrightarrow Q,</md>
</p>

<p>one may regard <m>P</m> as lying below <m>Q</m>.</p>

<p>Then conjunction behaves like meet:</p>

<p>
<md>P\wedge Q,</md>
</p>

<p>and disjunction behaves like join:</p>

<p>
<md>P\vee Q.</md>
</p>

<p>Boolean algebras model classical propositional logic.</p>

<p>Other lattice-like structures model intuitionistic and nonclassical logics.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-algebra">
<title>Lattice Theory and Algebra</title>
<p>Many algebraic structures naturally produce lattices.</p>

<p><em>Manual conversion required:</em> This table from the LaTeX source still needs to be converted to native PreTeXt.</p>

<p>Lattice theory therefore provides a common language for studying systems of
subobjects.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-data">
<title>Lattice Theory and Data</title>
<p>Order structures also appear in data analysis and knowledge representation.</p>

<p>For example, formal concept analysis organizes relationships between
objects and attributes into a <em>concept lattice</em>.</p>

<p>Concept lattices reveal hierarchical patterns and dependencies within data.</p>

<p>This is one example of lattice theory extending beyond traditional pure
algebra.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-hierarchy">
<title>A Hierarchy of Lattice Structures</title>
<p>Several important classes fit into the hierarchy</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=8mm,
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners,align=center,inner sep=5pt},
    &gt;=stealth
]
\node[box] (poset) {Posets};
\node[box,below=of poset] (lattice) {Lattices};
\node[box,below=of lattice] (bounded) {Bounded lattices};
\node[box,below left=of bounded] (modular) {Modular lattices};
\node[box,below right=of bounded] (comp) {Complemented lattices};
\node[box,below=18mm of bounded] (dist) {Distributive lattices};
\node[box,below=of dist] (bool) {Boolean algebras};</p>

<p>\draw[-&gt;] (lattice)--(poset);
\draw[-&gt;] (bounded)--(lattice);
\draw[-&gt;] (modular)--(lattice);
\draw[-&gt;] (dist)--(modular);
\draw[-&gt;] (comp)--(bounded);
\draw[-&gt;] (bool)--(dist);
\draw[-&gt;] (bool)--(comp);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>

<p>The diagram indicates implications, not a complete classification of every
possible relationship.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-every-poset-is-a-lattice">
<title>Assuming Every Poset Is a Lattice</title>
<p>A lattice requires every pair to possess both a meet and a join.</p>

<p>A general poset may lack either.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-minimal-with-least">
<title>Confusing Minimal with Least</title>
<p>A minimal element has nothing strictly below it.</p>

<p>A least element lies below every element.</p>

<p>There may be many minimal elements but at most one least element.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-maximal-with-greatest">
<title>Confusing Maximal with Greatest</title>
<p>A maximal element has nothing strictly above it.</p>

<p>A greatest element lies above every element.</p>

<p>These notions are not equivalent.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-meet-means-minimum">
<title>Assuming Meet Means Minimum</title>
<p>The meet</p>

<p>
<md>x\wedge y</md>
</p>

<p>is the greatest lower bound of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.</p>

<p>If <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are incomparable, the meet may be different from both.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-join-means-maximum">
<title>Assuming Join Means Maximum</title>
<p>The join</p>

<p>
<md>x\vee y</md>
</p>

<p>is the least upper bound.</p>

<p>It need not equal <m>x</m> or <m>y</m>.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-reading-a-hasse-diagram-as-an-ordinary-graph">
<title>Reading a Hasse Diagram as an Ordinary Graph</title>
<p>Edges in a Hasse diagram represent covering relations.</p>

<p>Transitive order relations are implied even when no edge is drawn.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-including-transitive-edges-in-a-hasse-diagram">
<title>Including Transitive Edges in a Hasse Diagram</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>x&lt;y&lt;z,</md>
</p>

<p>the relation <m>x&lt;z</m> is already implied.</p>

<p>A Hasse diagram normally does not draw a direct edge from <m>x</m> to <m>z</m>.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-every-bounded-lattice-is-complemented">
<title>Assuming Every Bounded Lattice Is Complemented</title>
<p>Having <m>0</m> and <m>1</m> does not guarantee that every element has a
complement.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-complements-are-always-unique">
<title>Assuming Complements Are Always Unique</title>
<p>Complements need not be unique in arbitrary complemented lattices.</p>

<p>They are unique in Boolean algebras because distributivity is available.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-modular-means-distributive">
<title>Assuming Modular Means Distributive</title>
<p>Every distributive lattice is modular, but not every modular lattice is
distributive.</p>

<p>The lattice <m>M_3</m> is the standard counterexample.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-a-lattice-is-automatically-complete">
<title>Assuming a Lattice Is Automatically Complete</title>
<p>A lattice only guarantees binary meets and joins.</p>

<p>A complete lattice requires meets and joins for every subset.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-lattice-ideals-with-ring-ideals">
<title>Confusing Lattice Ideals with Ring Ideals</title>
<p>The terminology is related historically and structurally, but the
definitions are different.</p>

<p>A lattice ideal is a downward-closed subset closed under joins.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-every-monotone-map-is-a-lattice-homomorphism">
<title>Assuming Every Monotone Map Is a Lattice Homomorphism</title>
<p>A monotone map preserves order.</p>

<p>A lattice homomorphism must preserve both meet and join.</p>

<p>The latter is a stronger requirement.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-applications">
<title>Applications</title>
<p>\begin{applicationbox}
<em>Abstract Algebra.</em>
Subgroups, ideals, submodules, subspaces, and congruences naturally form
lattices under inclusion.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Universal Algebra.</em>
Congruence lattices encode how an algebra can be partitioned into compatible
quotient structures.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Logic.</em>
Meet, join, and complement model conjunction, disjunction, and negation.
Boolean algebras provide an algebraic model of classical propositional
logic.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Set Theory.</em>
Power sets are complete Boolean lattices under inclusion, intersection,
union, and complement.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Linear Algebra.</em>
Subspaces form modular lattices, with intersection as meet and subspace sum
as join.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Topology.</em>
Open sets form complete lattice-like structures called frames, linking
lattice theory with point-free topology.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Combinatorics.</em>
Finite lattices interact with chains, antichains, rank functions, Boolean
lattices, Möbius inversion, and incidence structures.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Computer Science.</em>
Complete lattices and fixed-point theorems are used in program semantics,
static analysis, recursive definitions, databases, and formal verification.</p>

<p>\medskip</p>

<p><em>Data Analysis.</em>
Formal concept analysis uses concept lattices to organize relationships
between objects and attributes.
\end{applicationbox}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-15-lattice-theory-subsec-lattice-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
